WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.190
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by the Royal Air Force and the Briggs

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.900
Automotive Company, who are currently holding
an engineering design competition for Young

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.120
Engineers.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.960
One of the moments that will always stand
out in my life is the watching the joy and

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.050
excitement of the engineers at JPL celebrating
the monumental task they had completed.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.860
They had just landed a 900 kilogram rover
the size of a mini cooper on another planet.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.470
The Mars rover is bursting at the seams with
engineering innovation.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.050
I could detail the insane landing process
and any of the multitude of engineering marvels

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.989
on board, but today I want to discuss something
you may not have put much thought into.

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:40.069
Something we use on earth everyday.

00:00:40.069 --> 00:00:41.069
Tires.

00:00:41.069 --> 00:00:44.879
The wheels of the Mars Rover have been one
the biggest technical difficulties encountered

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:46.100
on the mission.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.670
Accruing substantial damage though the rough
Martian terrain.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.749
Surely the engineers at NASA could have predicted
this and designed something better.

00:00:53.749 --> 00:00:56.609
Well not everything is as simple as it may
and seem.

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:01.979
Even on flat hard ground, the rover has a
top speed of just 0.15 kilometres per hour.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:06.229
Yet this snail's pace still had enough force
to tear holes into the aluminium wheels of

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:07.229
the rover.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:10.950
The engineers have some strict criteria that
made the job more difficult.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.159
The wheels need to be stiff enough to support
the weight of the rover, at nearly 1 tonne

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.960
that isn’t an easy task.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.979
The wheels need to be as light as possible
to reduce launch costs and must to be able

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.579
maintain traction and navigate the unpredictable
martian terrain.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:28.800
The current record holder for longest journey
on an extraterrestrial body is held by the

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.070
Opportunity Rover at 45 kilometres, with the
previous record being the Russian Lunokhod

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.479
2, a lunar rover.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:39.320
Curiosity has racked up just 20 kilometres
to date [1].

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.299
All Mars rovers have used solid aluminium
wheels.

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:46.969
The Sojourner, Opportunity and Spirit rovers
all used the design successfully, but the

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:49.409
curiosity rover is much heavier.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:54.320
Each wheel on the Curiosity rover is about
half a metre in diameter and 400 millimeters

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.320
wide.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.899
Milled from a solid block of aluminium in
a similar fashion to the methods described

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:03.659
in my Bloodhound SSC video, but unlike the
Bloodhound, the Mars rover wheels are milled

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:08.500
down to just 0.75 millimeters thick over the
majority of its circumference.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.580
Damage here isn’t all that surprising, 0.75
millimeters thick is the same thickness as

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.980
a credit card.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.210
Running a nearly 1 tonne vehicle over a sharp
rock would be expected to damage aluminium

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.330
that thick.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:24.670
NASA simply underestimated the roughness of
the terrain, more worryingly though the load

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:30.120
bearing threads which are about 6.4 millimeters
thick are also damaged, and if they continue

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.740
to break the wheels will become useless.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.920
The Mars Rover cost 2.5 billion dollars to
develop and it employs a nuclear reactor for

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:38.920
power.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.090
Extending the life of this project was top
priority for it’s engineers, and so finding

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.910
a solution to this problem has been fairly
high on NASA’s list for future projects.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.260
So what other designs could we use?

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.720
To reduce the chances of the wheels experiencing
a stress that could damage them we want them

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.700
to be able to bend and conform to the terrain.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.880
This is what our rubber air filled tires do
on earth.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.150
Land rovers drive over similarly rough terrain
without any major problem, so why not use

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.430
tires like this on Mars?

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:07.430
Flip down of temperature with suitable image/footage
of Mars.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.700
Temperatures on Mars can dip as low as minus
130 degrees celsius.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.890
Temperatures this low would transform rubber
from an elastic material capable of absorbing

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.950
stress to a brittle glass-like material, making
it useless for this application.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:26.110
Rubber would also degrade from the UV radiation
it would be exposed to on the surface of mars.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:31.040
On top of this, weight savings is also top
priority and rubber wheels with thick rubber,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.720
steel reinforcement and a rim, are actually
quite heavy.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.540
The lunar rover used flexible steel mesh wheels,
with a stiffer inner frame to prevent over

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.330
deflection and thin strips of metal attached
to prevent the wheel from sinking into the

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.540
lunar soil.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.760
This option was studied for applications on
Mars, but the Lunar Rover had a mass of just

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.280
450 kilograms compared to the 900 kilograms
of the Curiosity Rover, combine this with

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.110
the higher gravity on Mars and it made the
wheel unsuitable for the application.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:04.150
The wheels would simply not be able to hold
the weight of the vehicle without deforming

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.770
permanently, but Goodyear, who developed the
original Lunar wheel, has been working alongside

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:14.710
NASA and in 2010 they were given the R&amp;D 100
award for this spring tyre, which could be

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:16.940
the solution to our problems.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:22.090
Being both light, capable of bending and conforming
to the terrain without permanently deforming

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:25.130
and capable of holding the weight of a heavier
rover.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.630
So what is it’s secret.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.540
What makes these spring tyres different to
the ones used on the Lunar Rover.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:34.350
They use a new age material that has some
incredible properties, not just for applications

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.000
in space, but right here on earth.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.380
This spring tyre uses a material called Nitinol.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.490
Nitinol is a nickel titanium alloy that has
been named a shape memory alloy, and for good

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.490
reason.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.030
You can bend and deform it, and then just
apply a little bit of heat and it magically

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.090
returns to its original shape.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.810
It remembers its original shape.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:59.070
That is incredible, but nothing is magic in
this world and everything has an explanation.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.450
Here is how it works.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:03.880
Typically when a material is stressed one
of two things can happen.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.590
If the stress is below it’s yield point,
the material will deform elastically like

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.540
a rubber band, and return to its original
shape when the stress is released.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.800
Alternatively, if the material is stressed
beyond its yield point the material will deform

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.150
permanently and when the stress is released
the material will be not return to its original

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.150
shape.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.450
So how does this permanent deformation occur?

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:27.920
If bonds are not broken, the material should
return to its original preferred crystal lattice

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.750
orientation, but when sufficient force is
applied small defects in the crystal lattice

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.450
are able to move.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.020
This is what is happening to the wheels of
the Mars Rover.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.100
When driving over pointed rocks and gravel
the stress exceeds the yield stress, and thus

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.280
the wheels are gradually picking up permanent
damage, and eventually cracks will form and

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.460
the material will fracture.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.510
Deformation occurs slightly differently for
Nitinol, as it has some unique properties

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.410
due to the internal crystal structure.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:56.860
When Nitinol is below a certain temperature
it has a crystal structure called martensite.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:01.330
It’s crystal structure is arranged in such
a way that it can accommodate deformation

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:02.370
very easily.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:06.910
Martensitic nitinol forms grains of twinned
atoms where the direction aligns.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:12.200
When stress is applied these twin grains deform
and align to best absorb the stress.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.960
Particular twin grains can grow at the expense
of others.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.380
This is called detwinning.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:21.120
This, like the dislocation movement in other
metals is permanent, without external energy

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.730
providing the energy needed to revert backwards,
but Nitinol can get that energy from heat.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:31.240
Upon heating the nitinol forms austenite,
an ordered and regular crystal structure,

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.880
which effectively resets the crystal structure,
and when the nitinol cools again the nitinol

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.630
remembers its original shape.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:42.080
What makes nitinol even more amazing and useful,
is that this transformation temperature can

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.030
be tailored for different applications.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:48.740
In general, increasing the titanium content
of the metal will increase the transformation

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:49.880
temperature.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.210
This is an incredibly useful property and
it has found many applications across many

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.210
industries.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.330
Let’s say we want the metal to remember
its shape at 37 degrees celsius, body temperature.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.800
Can you think of any applications when this
would be useful?

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:03.889
I’ll give you one.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:08.139
Traditionally when stents, which are tiny
tubes used to clear blockages in arteries,

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:12.270
are being deployed, a small balloon is used
to inflate it and plastically deform the stent

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:13.270
into shape.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:17.560
However this often places force on the lining
of the blood vessel, which can damage it and

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.530
cause it to form scar tissue.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.290
Many stent designs now employ nitinol which
has been tailored to remember it’s shape

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:28.710
at 37 degrees celsius, and so when unsheathed
it automatically expands into place without

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.980
placing excessive force on the blood vessel.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:31.980
[7]

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.370
So how is this useful for the Mars rover?

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.050
Do we have to apply heat to the metal so it
can recover its shape after running over a

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.480
particularly big rock?

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:43.260
No, luckily nitinol can also be induced into
that martensite to austenite transformation

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:47.650
through stress and strain, and so when the
stress passes a threshold it actually causes

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.669
the crystal lattice to transform to austenite,
and when the stress is released it returns

00:07:51.669 --> 00:07:54.680
to martensite and the metal recovers its shape.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:58.771
This is called super elasticity and it is
the property that allows these new Mars rover

00:07:58.771 --> 00:08:02.960
wheels to deform right down to the rim and
still recover its shape after.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.950
This combined with the interlocking coil design
allows the tyre to tolerate strains in a way

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:11.389
no other tyre could, while surviving the harsh
Martian environment.

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:15.130
These tyres could appear in NASA’s designs
for future Mars Rovers, like those planned

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:16.550
for launch in 2020.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:21.120
It’s easy to think something as simple as
a wheel will never be reinvented, but there

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.139
are always ideas and opportunities for people
willing to put the work into thinking about

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:27.139
them.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:30.080
That’s why I have teamed up with the Royal
Air Force and the Briggs Automotive Company

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.990
to bring you an exciting design competition
for Young Engineers.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.509
We want you to brainstorm designs for a new
Mars Transport Vehicle that improves on previous

00:08:38.509 --> 00:08:40.159
designs in some way.

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.759
We want your most innovative and creative
ideas.

00:08:42.759 --> 00:08:46.569
Think from the ground up, what problems will
Mars rovers encounter, have you ever thought

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:49.480
about how we communicate with a vehicle on
another planet.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.800
The top prize winner will have their idea
developed and made into a 3D model by the

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.149
Briggs Automotive Company.

00:08:55.149 --> 00:09:00.110
They’ll also win work experience at the
pioneering BAC factory in Liverpool that produces

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:03.450
the world’s only road legal single-seater
supercar.

